{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbajFGG8sk3B6EDucoB3", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main thing to know is that `finalize()` is not idempotent. The code confirms the stored hazard memory.\n\n- **Double-posting:** `finalize()` in `src/billing/invoices.py:21` POSTs to the external ledger (`LEDGER_URL`) with no idempotency key. If you call it twice, the invoice is posted twice.\n- **No retries:** Don't wrap it in retry logic. A request that times out after the ledger has already recorded the posting would be retried and double-posted. The call has a 10-second timeout, so this can happen.\n- **Use `finalize_once()`:** It's at line 29 and skips the call if `invoice.ledger_ref` is already set. Prefer it for any new call site.\n- **Remaining gap:** `finalize_once()` only checks the local `ledger_ref`. That value is only set after the ledger responds successfully. If the POST succeeds on the ledger side but the response is lost, `ledger_ref` stays empty. A later `finalize_once()` would then post again. Fixing that properly needs an idempotency key sent to the ledger, which depends on whether the ledger supports one. I haven't checked that.\n- **No concurrency guard:** Two concurrent `finalize_once()` calls on the same invoice can both pass the `ledger_ref` check. Nothing in this code prevents that.\n\nI didn't look for other callers of `finalize()` beyond this file. I'll check for direct calls that bypass `finalize_once()` once you tell me what you're changing."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1127, "cache_read_input_tokens": 21504, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1127}, "output_tokens": 496, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_man